== ws
set up the server and the Mac that works on it

Usage:
  ws [flags]
  ws [command]

Available Commands:
  client      the commands for a Mac
  help        Help about any command
  server      the commands for an Ubuntu server
  unit        plan and apply the units of ws's kinds, KIND/NAME, for mise
  version     print the version of this ws

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for ws

Use "ws [command] --help" for more information about a command.
== ws client
the commands for a Mac

Usage:
  ws client [flags]
  ws client [command]

Available Commands:
  cable       give the cable's service its Manual Configuration of the Mac's end
  cld         install cld into ~/.local/bin, and keep it at its latest release
  git         the commands of git's units
  keys        copy the .pub of each key of Secretive's that values.yml names into /etc/ws
  networks    route each server's WS_TUNNEL_NETWORKS and send its WS_RESOLVERS' domains to it
  remove      remove a server from the Mac
  resolver    send DOMAIN's names to the nameservers, or drop it
  secrets     the commands of the secrets repository, which fnox keeps
  servers     list the Mac's servers with their target, slot and tunnel
  ssh         the commands of ssh's units
  tunnel      the Mac's tunnel to ws
  values      print the Mac's values

Flags:
  -h, --help            help for client
      --server string   the server to act on, which may be left out where there is one

Use "ws client [command] --help" for more information about a command.
== ws client cable
The service that a server's WS_CABLE_SERVICE names takes 10.77.0.2/30 with no router, through sudo, where networksetup shows other settings. A service networksetup lacks, or one with a router outside the cable's /30, as an uplink has, fails it.

--check fails where a change is due, and changes nothing.

Usage:
  ws client cable [flags]

Flags:
      --check   fail where a change is due, and change nothing
  -h, --help    help for cable

Global Flags:
      --server string   the server to act on, which may be left out where there is one
== ws client cld
It installs cld with the install.sh of its latest release where it is missing, and runs cld update where a newer release is out. A cld built from a clone is left as it is. A failed install or update, or a latest release that it cannot read, is a warning.

--check fails where a change is due, and changes nothing.

Usage:
  ws client cld [flags]

Flags:
      --check   fail where a change is due, and change nothing
  -h, --help    help for cld

Global Flags:
      --server string   the server to act on, which may be left out where there is one
== ws client git
the commands of git's units

Usage:
  ws client git [flags]
  ws client git [command]

Available Commands:
  included    say by the exit status whether the git config on stdin includes /etc/ws/gitconfig at its top

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for git

Global Flags:
      --server string   the server to act on, which may be left out where there is one

Use "ws client git [command] --help" for more information about a command.
== ws client git included
It reads the text as git does. It exits 0 where the text includes /etc/ws/gitconfig once, at its top, and 1 where it lacks the include. It exits 2 where the include sits below the file's own settings or comes twice, which it would then override, or where git cannot read the text. git's unit of the include runs it.

Usage:
  ws client git included [flags]

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for included

Global Flags:
      --server string   the server to act on, which may be left out where there is one
== ws client keys
ssh and git name a key that Secretive's agent holds by its .pub, which macOS keeps ws from reading in Secretive's container. So ws keeps a copy, from the agent, in /etc/ws/secretive. A key in a file has none.

--check fails where a change is due, and changes nothing.

Usage:
  ws client keys [flags]

Flags:
      --check   fail where a change is due, and change nothing
  -h, --help    help for keys

Global Flags:
      --server string   the server to act on, which may be left out where there is one
== ws client networks
Each server with WS_PUBLIC_KEY gets its routes.d/values, and a file in /etc/resolver for each domain of its WS_RESOLVERS, written through sudo. A file of ws's in /etc/resolver that no server names goes. A file of a domain that ws did not write fails it.

--check fails where a change is due, and changes nothing.

Usage:
  ws client networks [flags]

Flags:
      --check   fail where a change is due, and change nothing
  -h, --help    help for networks

Global Flags:
      --server string   the server to act on, which may be left out where there is one
== ws client remove
It first takes NAME's host key, from known_hosts, out of the secrets repository by git and gh: from the recipients of fnox.toml and of every profile file, whose values it encrypts again with the Nano, by a pull request, and from the repository's deploy keys. Where that fails, nothing else changes. Where known_hosts holds no key of NAME, where this Mac has no fnox key, or with --skip-secrets, as where the secrets are out of reach, it leaves them and prints what to do there by hand. It then deletes servers/NAME beside values.yml and NAME's entry in known_hosts, and leaves the server as it is. mise's bootstrap then takes its daemon, routes and host off. It refuses the Mac's last server, as the bootstrap takes a Mac with none for a stray WS_VALUES and prunes no daemon, and takes no --server: the argument is the consent.

Usage:
  ws client remove NAME [flags]

Flags:
  -h, --help           help for remove
      --skip-secrets   leave NAME's host key in the secrets, and print it to drop by hand

Global Flags:
      --server string   the server to act on, which may be left out where there is one
== ws client resolver
It has macOS ask the nameservers for DOMAIN, or drop the file of DOMAIN where it gets none.

Usage:
  ws client resolver DOMAIN [ADDRESS...] [flags]

Flags:
      --check   print each change and make none
  -h, --help    help for resolver

Global Flags:
      --server string   the server to act on, which may be left out where there is one
== ws client secrets
the commands of the secrets repository, which fnox keeps

Usage:
  ws client secrets [flags]
  ws client secrets [command]

Available Commands:
  add         add a server's host key to the secrets, and run the bootstrap with them there
  init        set the secrets repository up with the Nano and a backup key

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for secrets

Global Flags:
      --server string   the server to act on, which may be left out where there is one

Use "ws client secrets [command] --help" for more information about a command.
== ws client secrets add
It takes the ed25519 host key of the server NAME from known_hosts. Where known_hosts holds none, it scans the server, prints the key's fingerprint, and records the key once you say that the server's console prints the same. It reads the server's hostname by ssh. In a temporary clone of the secrets repository, it makes the key the hostname's recipient in fnox.toml and in fnox.HOSTNAME.toml, and encrypts their values again, which asks for the Nano's PIN and a touch. It keeps the key as the repository's read-only deploy key, titled with the hostname, and lands the change by a pull request once its checks pass. It then runs the server's task secrets over ssh -t. Last, it prints each host key that the secrets hold and known_hosts gives no host, to drop by hand where no server holds it now. Each step changes nothing where it is done, so run it again where one fails.

Usage:
  ws client secrets add NAME [flags]

Flags:
      --address string   the server's address, where ssh reaches it before the tunnel is up
  -h, --help             help for add
      --port int         the server's ssh port at --address, where it is not 22

Global Flags:
      --server string   the server to act on, which may be left out where there is one
== ws client secrets init
It reads the repository from WS_SECRETS_REPO in /opt/ws/mise.toml, and clones it. It takes the age identity that the YubiKey Nano holds, or makes one, which asks for its PIN and a touch, and keeps its stub in fnox's key file. Where fnox.toml is missing, it makes a backup key, starts fnox.toml with both recipients, and prints the backup's secret line once. It declares each input of /opt/ws's server roles that fnox.toml lacks, empty. It lands the change by a pull request once its checks pass, and opens none where nothing changed. It stops where a pull request of an earlier run is still open. Run it again once /opt/ws holds a release that declares a new input.

Usage:
  ws client secrets init [flags]

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for init

Global Flags:
      --server string   the server to act on, which may be left out where there is one
== ws client servers
It lists each server of servers/ beside values.yml: its target, its slot, and its tunnel, which is absent, stopped, starting, unreadable, or the tier and endpoint it holds. A server whose file ws refuses shows - and its errors follow the list, with exit code 1. It reads alone, as the user, and takes no --server.

Usage:
  ws client servers [flags]

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for servers

Global Flags:
      --server string   the server to act on, which may be left out where there is one
